All,

This is not about the Acme Philmac.

We have had recent experience with the use of the 2G 2x2 Tee, and 2" Elbow =
in pulled pipe installations that require a different approach than was use=
d with the 3G fittings.

In the pulled pipe installation scenario the tee or elbow has to be assembl=
ed on to the ends of the pipe as they present themselves in the "pot hole" =
they were pulled through.  In one method of installation the ends of the pi=
pe are cut to be the right length.  The fitting is loaded on one pipe and t=
hen the next pipe is deflected and "popped" into its socket and so on.  The=
 effect of this method is that the pipe end can come in at an angle and cat=
ch and damage the seal that is affixed to the seal tube.  This will result =
in a leak.  The tee is worse than the elbow.  This will not apply to the ac=
me fittings because they have the advantage of being no stop repair style f=
ittings which can be slid on one end and then back over the joint without a=
 need to deflect and "pop" the pipe in.

A different method is required to install these fittings in this situation.=
  One method is to use the "plan b" for the acme tee install.  Remove the n=
ut assemblies and load them and the body separately on to the pipe ends and=
 then assemble the nuts on to the fittings tightening as required.

A second method that can work is one that has been used with PVC laterals. =
 The pipe is pushed out a ways towards the adjacent pot hole allowing room =
to orient the pipe straight on to enter the socket in the normal attitude.

You all will have to contact active projects and alert your customers as so=
on as possible.  Do not wait for us to come up with more elegant visual aid=
s.

If you have questions about this don't hesitate to call me.

Mike and I have done enough bitchin' and cussin' for everyone so let's not =
waste time grinding on the obvious.

J

